<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giovanni Battista Cairato</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th century</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giovanni Battista Cairato was an Italian military architect from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> who served Philip II as chief engineer of the Portuguese possessions in Asia from the 1580s. He designed or rebuilt fortifications across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-da-india">Estado da Índia</a>, the Portuguese state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-jesus">Fort Jesus</a> at Mombasa, begun in 1593, which survives as one of the finest examples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> military architecture.</p></div>
